WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:06.039 line:0%
The past year has been
and continues to be

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:08.808 line:0%
an incredibly stressful time
for just about everybody.

00:00:08.841 --> 00:00:11.411 line:0%
But for a lot
of white Americans like me,

00:00:11.444 --> 00:00:14.514
it's also been a year of
recognizing just how much harder

00:00:14.547 --> 00:00:16.516
that stress is hitting
black and brown Americans.

00:00:20.686 --> 00:00:24.457
The COVID-19 pandemic
put a spotlight on the racial
disparities in health.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:27.326
In the U.S., the death rate
among black people

00:00:27.359 --> 00:00:29.562
is twice as high
compared to white people.

00:00:29.595 --> 00:00:31.798 line:0%
Black Americans in particular

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:34.834 line:0%
are dying at double the rate
of their population.

00:00:34.867 --> 00:00:38.805
Most of the bus drivers
in the city of Chicago
are black.

00:00:38.838 --> 00:00:42.341
Most of the food service
workers are Latino.

00:00:42.374 --> 00:00:45.411
Those populations are struggling
hard in all of this.

00:00:45.444 --> 00:00:47.680
But the disproportionate effect
of this virus

00:00:47.713 --> 00:00:51.384
is just one part of a long list
of racial gaps in health.

00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:54.220
Would I be able to get
a subway map?

00:00:54.253 --> 00:00:56.622 align:start size:63% position:38%
African Americans
are at greater risk

00:00:56.655 --> 00:00:58.624 align:start size:63% position:38%
for developing
chronic diseases.

00:00:58.657 --> 00:01:02.195
Reporter: More than
75% of black Americans
have hypertension,

00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:05.098
compared with less than
50% of whites.

00:01:05.131 --> 00:01:07.200
And when we talk about
why those gaps exist,

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:08.701
we often talk about
neighborhood,

00:01:08.734 --> 00:01:11.571
socioeconomics,
and medical treatments.

00:01:11.604 --> 00:01:14.440
But I'm wondering about
the experience of racism itself.

00:01:14.473 --> 00:01:16.576
<i> How does the stress
 of living with discrimination</i>

00:01:16.609 --> 00:01:18.811
<i> impact people's bodies?</i>

00:01:18.844 --> 00:01:22.748
Your age captures
how long you have lived.

00:01:22.781 --> 00:01:25.251
But if you're living
in a bad environment,

00:01:25.284 --> 00:01:29.288
your age is also capturing
how long you have been exposed

00:01:29.321 --> 00:01:31.424
<i> to bad environmental
 conditions</i>

00:01:31.457 --> 00:01:34.827
<i> and how physiologically
 deteriorated</i>

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.530
you have become as a result
of that exposure.

00:01:37.563 --> 00:01:41.601 align:start position:0%
These microaggressions,
they accumulate over time.

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:44.637 align:start position:0%
It's like a death by
a thousand tiny little cuts.

00:01:49.575 --> 00:01:52.779 line:0%
<i> ( theme music playing )</i>

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:02.922 align:start position:0% line:0%
So, I have this map.

00:02:02.955 --> 00:02:05.658 align:start position:0% line:0%
What I would love to do
is map out

00:02:05.691 --> 00:02:08.928 align:start position:0%
what life expectancy
looks like in New York,

00:02:08.961 --> 00:02:10.496
depending on your neighborhood.

00:02:10.529 --> 00:02:11.797 align:start size:16% position:84%
Okay.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:13.633 align:start size:84% position:16%
- That kind of works.
- Okay.

00:02:15.734 --> 00:02:18.337 size:81% position:41%
So, we're gonna use green
for anything that is above

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:21.407 align:start position:0%
the New York City
average of 81.2,

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:22.742 align:start position:0%
and pink for anything
that is below.

00:02:22.775 --> 00:02:24.944 align:start size:59% position:41%
Okay, so what is
Washington Heights?

00:02:24.977 --> 00:02:26.345 align:start position:0%
84.4.

00:02:26.378 --> 00:02:28.414 align:start position:0%
East Harlem is 77.9.

00:02:28.447 --> 00:02:30.283 align:start size:34% position:66%
77.9

00:02:31.951 --> 00:02:34.353 align:start position:0%
So, Upper East Side,
that's where we are right now.

00:02:34.386 --> 00:02:36.556
- 86.4.
- Okay.

00:02:36.589 --> 00:02:39.926
And this already is, like,
almost a ten-year difference.

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:41.727 align:start size:75% position:25%
Yeah,
it's a stark difference.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.932 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Thank you.
- 86.3, Murray Hill.

00:02:45.965 --> 00:02:48.801 align:start position:0%
One thing I think is really
interesting with these numbers

00:02:48.834 --> 00:02:51.971 align:start position:0%
is, like,
if the Upper East Side
were its own country,

00:02:52.004 --> 00:02:54.273 align:start position:0%
it would be like
the second best place to live,

00:02:54.306 --> 00:02:55.675 size:84% position:42%
for longevity in the world.

00:02:55.708 --> 00:02:58.511 align:start size:75% position:25%
But then look at this.
As you're clustered here

00:02:58.544 --> 00:03:01.480 align:start size:88% position:13%
- in East Harlem
  and Central Harlem...
- Yeah.

00:03:01.513 --> 00:03:04.951 align:start size:88% position:13%
...is not a good place
in terms of life expectancy.

00:03:04.984 --> 00:03:07.820 align:start position:0%
Right, and people
in a majority white area

00:03:07.853 --> 00:03:09.655 align:start position:0%
are living almost
a decade longer

00:03:09.688 --> 00:03:12.758 align:start position:0%
than people in
a majority non-white area.

00:03:12.791 --> 00:03:16.662 align:start position:0%
Huge difference.
Can you explain why that is?

00:03:16.695 --> 00:03:18.731 align:start size:56% position:44%
Think about
the socio-economic
differences

00:03:18.764 --> 00:03:19.999 align:start size:56% position:44%
between these
two communities.

00:03:20.032 --> 00:03:22.401 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah.
- That difference in income

00:03:22.434 --> 00:03:26.372 align:start size:69% position:31%
would affect access
to quality healthcare,
food, transportation.

00:03:26.405 --> 00:03:30.343 align:start size:59% position:41%
All of those
factors play a role
in life expectancy.

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:32.478
Yeah.

00:03:32.511 --> 00:03:36.816 align:start position:0%
So, does socio-economic status
explain the whole picture?

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:39.652 align:start size:66% position:34%
No. I may be stressed
for other reasons.

00:03:39.685 --> 00:03:41.354
You know,
I'm African-American,

00:03:41.387 --> 00:03:43.889 align:start position:0%
maybe I'm having stressors
in my workplace,

00:03:43.922 --> 00:03:47.860 align:start position:0%
or maybe I'm having stressors
finding a quality
health provider,

00:03:47.893 --> 00:03:50.429 align:start position:0%
or maybe I feel like
I'm being discriminated against.

00:03:50.462 --> 00:03:54.367 align:start position:0%
Whereas, you know,
someone that is not a minority

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.771 align:start position:0%
in the same socio-economic
status may not have
those same stressors.

00:04:03.575 --> 00:04:06.412 align:start position:0% line:0%
So, in the U.S.,
your race plays a huge part

00:04:06.445 --> 00:04:08.547 align:start position:0% line:0%
in how long
you can expect to live.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:11.751 align:start position:0% line:0%
If you were Hispanic,
you can expect to live
to almost 82.

00:04:11.784 --> 00:04:13.886
Almost 79, if you are white.

00:04:13.919 --> 00:04:16.389
About 75, if you're black.

00:04:16.422 --> 00:04:17.890
That is a three-year
age difference

00:04:17.923 --> 00:04:20.026
between Hispanic people
and white people,

00:04:20.059 --> 00:04:24.430
and a four-year gap
between black people
and white people.

00:04:24.463 --> 00:04:27.900 size:78% position:39%
So, even when you control
for education and income,

00:04:27.933 --> 00:04:30.703
even the health behaviors,
there's still a gap.

00:04:30.736 --> 00:04:33.439 align:start size:66% position:34%
I feel like
this all makes sense,

00:04:33.472 --> 00:04:37.443 align:start size:78% position:22%
it's just never something
easy for me to accept.

00:04:37.476 --> 00:04:40.880
So, those longevity counts

00:04:40.913 --> 00:04:44.784
don't really
explain the full story.

00:04:44.817 --> 00:04:46.485
So, if you look
at the percentage of people

00:04:46.518 --> 00:04:49.055
who report being in very good
or excellent health,

00:04:49.088 --> 00:04:52.425
among people who
self-identify as white

00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:54.593
and are also socially
assigned white,

00:04:54.626 --> 00:04:57.596
that number is 58%.

00:04:57.629 --> 00:05:00.066 align:start size:75% position:25%
What does it mean to be
socially assigned white?

00:05:00.099 --> 00:05:02.768 align:start position:0%
So, that just means people
perceive you as white.

00:05:02.801 --> 00:05:08.541 align:start position:0%
And for people who
self-identify and socially
categorized as Hispanic,

00:05:08.574 --> 00:05:11.410
the number is 47%.

00:05:11.443 --> 00:05:13.079 align:start position:0%
So, despite longer
life expectancy,

00:05:13.112 --> 00:05:16.082
fewer Hispanics report
being in good health.

00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:20.453
That difference right there
is the health gap.

00:05:20.486 --> 00:05:25.424
But if we look at people
who self-identify as Hispanic

00:05:25.457 --> 00:05:28.461
who pass as white,
their health outcomes

00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:31.797
are almost on par
with white people.

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:34.734
And we see that same effect
for Native Americans.

00:05:34.767 --> 00:05:37.470
Those who pass as white
have health outcomes

00:05:37.503 --> 00:05:41.006
that are almost on par
with white people.

00:05:41.039 --> 00:05:43.042 align:start position:0%
And that difference
is even more drastic

00:05:43.075 --> 00:05:46.812
for people
who are mixed race.

00:05:46.845 --> 00:05:49.849
So, if you are mixed race
but you are perceived as black,

00:05:49.882 --> 00:05:53.018 align:start position:0%
your health outcomes
wind up being much worse.

00:05:53.051 --> 00:05:57.990 align:start size:69% position:31%
My biggest takeaway
from this is just how
proximity to whiteness

00:05:58.023 --> 00:06:00.659 align:start size:69% position:31%
means that you're
gonna be better off.

00:06:00.692 --> 00:06:02.828 align:start size:81% position:19%
So that means that
if they go to the hospital

00:06:02.861 --> 00:06:05.831 align:start size:72% position:28%
or they're consulting
with some kind of
health professional,

00:06:05.864 --> 00:06:07.833 size:78% position:61%
they will probably
get better treatment.

00:06:07.866 --> 00:06:10.569 align:start size:72% position:28%
It's fascinating,
the way whiteness works

00:06:10.602 --> 00:06:12.671 align:start size:69% position:31%
when it comes
to health outcomes.

00:06:12.704 --> 00:06:14.140 align:start position:0%
It's like regardless
of your background,

00:06:14.173 --> 00:06:16.175 align:start position:0%
the way that you're seen
when you walk into a hospital

00:06:16.208 --> 00:06:17.610 align:start position:0%
or you're walking
down the street,

00:06:17.643 --> 00:06:19.578 align:start position:0%
that's what makes
the biggest difference.

00:06:19.611 --> 00:06:21.814 align:start size:66% position:34%
Right. This plays out
for me personally.

00:06:21.847 --> 00:06:26.118 align:start size:66% position:34%
I generally try
to avoid having to go
to the emergency room

00:06:26.151 --> 00:06:29.155 align:start size:84% position:16%
because I'm just
always afraid about, again,
how I'm gonna be perceived.

00:06:29.188 --> 00:06:32.525 align:start position:0%
Yeah, I never really
thought about how my race

00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:34.860 align:start position:0%
personally affected me
as I, you know,

00:06:34.893 --> 00:06:36.762 align:start position:0%
walk into a hospital room,
for example.

00:06:36.795 --> 00:06:38.531 size:88% position:44%
My absence of understanding,

00:06:38.564 --> 00:06:41.000 align:start position:0%
my absence of knowledge
is the point.

00:06:41.033 --> 00:06:46.105 align:start position:0%
That shows something about,
um, the privilege of ignorance

00:06:46.138 --> 00:06:49.475 align:start position:0%
and of unawareness
of these things that
I've been able to have.

00:06:49.508 --> 00:06:52.077 align:start position:0%
But discrimination varies
from place to place,

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:54.046
and that led me to this.

00:06:54.079 --> 00:06:56.715
In 2015, a team of researchers
led by this professor

00:06:56.748 --> 00:06:59.718
named David Chae
looked at the proportion

00:06:59.751 --> 00:07:02.188
of Google searches
across America

00:07:02.221 --> 00:07:05.558 align:start size:91% position:9%
- that used the "N" word.
- What? Okay.

00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:09.762 size:84% position:42%
Specifically the "N" word
ending in "E-R" or "E-R-S."

00:07:09.795 --> 00:07:12.198 align:start position:0%
So that it gets rid
of any music lyrics,
things like that.

00:07:12.231 --> 00:07:15.601 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Absolutely.
- It's not a perfect
  measure of racism,

00:07:15.634 --> 00:07:18.671
but it kind of provides,
like, a good proxy measure

00:07:18.704 --> 00:07:20.840 align:start position:0%
of, like, the people
who are more likely to do that

00:07:20.873 --> 00:07:23.142 align:start position:0%
are more likely to have
a negative racial sentiment.

00:07:23.175 --> 00:07:26.545 align:start position:0%
So what they found is
for every one unit increase

00:07:26.578 --> 00:07:29.648
of area racism
in each media market,

00:07:29.681 --> 00:07:33.586
that corresponded
with a 5.7% increase

00:07:33.619 --> 00:07:35.754
in black mortality.

00:07:35.787 --> 00:07:37.656 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Racism kills.
- Right.

00:07:37.689 --> 00:07:39.525 align:start size:69% position:31%
This is the first time
I'm hearing

00:07:39.558 --> 00:07:41.560 align:start size:72% position:28%
of racism being
quantified in this way.

00:07:41.593 --> 00:07:43.729 align:start size:63% position:38%
This is, like,
really interesting.

00:07:43.762 --> 00:07:45.931 align:start position:0%
These three different studies
seem to show

00:07:45.964 --> 00:07:49.835 align:start position:0%
that interpersonal racism
might be this overlooked factor

00:07:49.868 --> 00:07:52.538 align:start position:0%
in negative health outcomes
in this country.

00:07:52.571 --> 00:07:54.607 align:start size:84% position:16%
When someone says something
racist to you,

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:58.043 size:81% position:59%
it's a comment that goes
to the core of your being.

00:07:58.076 --> 00:08:00.779 align:start size:72% position:28%
Like, I cannot change
the pigment of my skin,
and it makes sense

00:08:00.812 --> 00:08:05.784 align:start size:97% position:3%
that with every single incident
that you have to deal with,

00:08:05.817 --> 00:08:07.887 size:88% position:56%
with every racist encounter,

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:09.622 align:start size:72% position:28%
like, it's taking years
off your life.

00:08:09.655 --> 00:08:11.957 align:start size:59% position:41%
That's crazy.

00:08:11.990 --> 00:08:13.626 align:start position:0%
Not to mention that
the perpetrators

00:08:13.659 --> 00:08:14.994 align:start position:0%
of those treatments
get to forget about it.

00:08:15.027 --> 00:08:17.796 align:start size:56% position:44%
Truly forget! Yes.

00:08:17.829 --> 00:08:19.632 align:start position:0%
And, you know, the people
on the receiving end

00:08:19.665 --> 00:08:21.634 align:start position:0%
of that treatment
don't get that privilege.

00:08:21.667 --> 00:08:22.534 align:start size:41% position:59%
Yeah.

00:08:26.605 --> 00:08:29.174
All right.

00:08:29.207 --> 00:08:31.710
We're about to call
David Williams.

00:08:31.743 --> 00:08:34.980
He's a professor at Harvard
who studies the relationship

00:08:35.013 --> 00:08:37.917
between race, racism,
and our health.

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:41.153 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Let's give him a call.
- ( Skype chiming )

00:08:41.186 --> 00:08:43.155
Hi, Dr. Williams.
How's it going?

00:08:43.188 --> 00:08:44.657 align:start size:50% position:50%
I am doing well.
How are you?

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:46.025 align:start position:0%
Pretty good.

00:08:46.058 --> 00:08:49.295 align:start position:0%
How does discrimination
influence people's health?

00:08:49.328 --> 00:08:52.298 line:0%
So, we have known
for a long time

00:08:52.331 --> 00:08:56.135 line:0%
that stressful life experiences
adversely affect health.

00:08:56.168 --> 00:08:59.171 align:start size:53% position:47%
And over the last
25 years or so,

00:08:59.204 --> 00:09:03.676 align:start size:69% position:31%
we have expanded
the types of stressful
life experiences

00:09:03.709 --> 00:09:07.846 align:start size:69% position:31%
that we study
to include experiences
of discrimination,

00:09:07.879 --> 00:09:12.017
little indignities
that seem to chip away

00:09:12.050 --> 00:09:15.854
at the well-being of individuals
on a day to day basis.

00:09:15.887 --> 00:09:18.857
What we've found
is persons who score high

00:09:18.890 --> 00:09:21.026
on experiences
of discrimination,

00:09:21.059 --> 00:09:23.729
they're more likely
to get diabetes,

00:09:23.762 --> 00:09:26.265
breast cancer, heart disease.

00:09:26.298 --> 00:09:29.868
And the cumulative impact
of the psycho-social stress

00:09:29.901 --> 00:09:33.205
and the economic stress
and the discrimination

00:09:33.238 --> 00:09:37.876
leads to physiological
deterioration, to weathering.

00:09:37.909 --> 00:09:41.213
They are literally
aging more rapidly

00:09:41.246 --> 00:09:44.683
than the rest of the population
in the United States.

00:09:44.716 --> 00:09:48.053
And how specifically
does the anticipation

00:09:48.086 --> 00:09:53.058
of a racist encounter impact
the health of people of color?

00:09:53.091 --> 00:09:58.230
Often, people are taking steps
before they even leave home

00:09:58.263 --> 00:10:02.401
to minimize the occurrence
of these experiences.

00:10:02.434 --> 00:10:05.904
And I'll be honest with you,
I feel physiologically

00:10:05.937 --> 00:10:09.908
my blood pressure goes up
any time I see a police car
driving behind me.

00:10:09.941 --> 00:10:11.910
And I've had good experiences
with the police,

00:10:11.943 --> 00:10:13.912
but I've had bad experiences
with the police.

00:10:13.945 --> 00:10:16.248
And it's that reality,
that threat,

00:10:16.281 --> 00:10:20.753
um, has a physiological
cost for me.

00:10:20.786 --> 00:10:24.390 align:start position:0%
And how does discrimination
affect people from one
generation to the next?

00:10:24.423 --> 00:10:26.992
There are studies of survivors
of the Holocaust

00:10:27.025 --> 00:10:31.196
that document changes
in gene expression

00:10:31.229 --> 00:10:33.932
that puts those persons
who had the experience

00:10:33.965 --> 00:10:37.836
at higher risk
of mental health problems,
such as PTSD.

00:10:37.869 --> 00:10:40.706
And that epigenetic change

00:10:40.739 --> 00:10:42.708
is evident also
in their offspring.

00:10:42.741 --> 00:10:46.879
We haven't done that work yet
in studies of discrimination,

00:10:46.912 --> 00:10:49.782
but women who report
everyday discrimination

00:10:49.815 --> 00:10:53.318
while they are pregnant
are more likely to give birth

00:10:53.351 --> 00:10:54.953
to lower birth weight infants.

00:10:54.986 --> 00:10:56.789
But after the child is born,

00:10:56.822 --> 00:11:00.259
there are a number of studies
that find experiences

00:11:00.292 --> 00:11:03.796
of discrimination by the father
or by the mother

00:11:03.829 --> 00:11:06.999
has negative effects
on the development of the child.

00:11:07.032 --> 00:11:10.436
And if we wanted to measure
the weathering effect

00:11:10.469 --> 00:11:13.472
over a lifetime,
how do we do that?

00:11:13.505 --> 00:11:15.874
Looking at allostatic load
is one way

00:11:15.907 --> 00:11:17.810
you can get a handle on that.

00:11:17.843 --> 00:11:21.413 align:start size:72% position:28% line:0%
What exactly
is the allostatic load?

00:11:21.446 --> 00:11:23.215 align:start position:0%
Christophe: You can think
of allostatic load

00:11:23.248 --> 00:11:26.151 align:start position:0%
like a measure
of all of the wear and tear

00:11:26.184 --> 00:11:28.220 align:start position:0%
on the body accumulated
over a lifetime.

00:11:28.253 --> 00:11:32.391 align:start position:0%
So, in a way,
allostatic load gives us
a proxy measure of aging.

00:11:32.424 --> 00:11:34.793 align:start position:0%
Now, to be clear,
some degree of stress is good,

00:11:34.826 --> 00:11:37.463 align:start position:0%
but it's when
stress becomes chronic

00:11:37.496 --> 00:11:40.065 align:start position:0%
that we start to see
really serious impacts
on our health.

00:11:40.098 --> 00:11:41.200 align:start size:63% position:38% line:0%
Lee: And that's
what we've got here.

00:11:41.233 --> 00:11:43.435 align:start position:0% line:0%
Christophe:
That's what we got here.

00:11:43.468 --> 00:11:45.170
So we've built out this chart
with some of the best data

00:11:45.203 --> 00:11:46.939 size:88% position:44%
that we can find out there
about the racial stress gap.

00:11:46.972 --> 00:11:49.174 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Should we get started?
- Let's do it.

00:11:49.207 --> 00:11:51.343 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Okay.
- Show me how it's done.

00:11:51.376 --> 00:11:53.345 align:start position:0%
So, on this chart,

00:11:53.378 --> 00:11:56.181 align:start position:0%
red lines are gonna
be white respondents,

00:11:56.214 --> 00:11:59.151 align:start position:0%
blue is black respondents
in this study.

00:11:59.184 --> 00:12:00.853
- You want me to hold this?
- You slap the magnet down.

00:12:00.886 --> 00:12:03.188 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
- Okay. Okay.
- And I'm gonna pull this.

00:12:03.221 --> 00:12:07.893 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
- So, what's five mean?
- Five is a very high
  allostatic load.

00:12:07.926 --> 00:12:10.863 line:0%
After four, that's
when we really start to see

00:12:10.896 --> 00:12:13.398 align:start size:78% position:22% line:0%
- differences in
  mortality rates.
- Wow. Okay.

00:12:13.431 --> 00:12:16.869 line:0%
So, it's 1.1
for white respondents

00:12:16.902 --> 00:12:19.304 line:0%
versus 1.6
for black respondents.

00:12:19.337 --> 00:12:21.907 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
- This is the group
  that we're in.
- Yeah.

00:12:21.940 --> 00:12:23.208 line:0%
( groans )

00:12:23.241 --> 00:12:25.778 align:start position:0%
Wow, that one's
really stretched.

00:12:29.347 --> 00:12:33.051 line:0%
So now, the average
white respondent has 1.8,

00:12:33.084 --> 00:12:36.522 line:0%
compared to the average
black respondent at 2.2.

00:12:36.555 --> 00:12:39.057 align:start position:0% line:0%
Just exposed to more stressors
as a black person.

00:12:39.090 --> 00:12:41.460 align:start position:0%
I mean, that's just what
the numbers are pointing to.

00:12:41.493 --> 00:12:46.064 align:start position:0%
The most anxious I feel
is walking home at night

00:12:46.097 --> 00:12:47.533 size:78% position:39%
walking into my building.

00:12:47.566 --> 00:12:50.235 align:start position:0%
Not to be that guy,
but as far as I know,

00:12:50.268 --> 00:12:51.937 size:81% position:41%
I'm the only black person
that lives in my building.

00:12:51.970 --> 00:12:54.206 align:start position:0%
I've had people ask me
when I come home with groceries

00:12:54.239 --> 00:12:57.943 size:75% position:38%
if I'm making a delivery
on one of these floors.

00:12:57.976 --> 00:13:01.246 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I hate that part of the day.
- That's stress.

00:13:01.279 --> 00:13:03.081 align:start size:72% position:28%
For what it's worth,
these numbers have been

00:13:03.114 --> 00:13:05.083 align:start size:69% position:31%
adjusted for
socio-economic status,

00:13:05.116 --> 00:13:06.919 line:0%
so the difference
that we're seeing here

00:13:06.952 --> 00:13:10.289 line:0%
is more or less
a racial difference.

00:13:10.322 --> 00:13:13.425 align:start position:0% line:0%
So, what happens
around 35 to 44?

00:13:13.458 --> 00:13:14.993 align:start size:91% position:9%
- 35 to 44?
- The age my siblings are.

00:13:15.026 --> 00:13:17.029
- Really?
- Yeah.

00:13:23.068 --> 00:13:25.504 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Now we're at the end here.
- Now we're at the end.

00:13:25.537 --> 00:13:27.005 align:start position:0%
These are my folks.

00:13:27.038 --> 00:13:31.176 align:start size:75% position:25%
White respondents had
an allostatic load of 4,

00:13:31.209 --> 00:13:34.246 align:start size:81% position:19%
black respondents had
an allostatic load of 4.8.

00:13:34.279 --> 00:13:36.949 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Jeez.
- I mean, this to me

00:13:36.982 --> 00:13:39.218 align:start size:63% position:38%
is the most striking
part of this data,

00:13:39.251 --> 00:13:42.287 align:start size:88% position:13%
is the fact that
black respondents were aging

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:46.024 align:start size:84% position:16%
a full decade faster
than the white respondents.

00:13:46.057 --> 00:13:49.061 align:start position:0%
Yeah, this is crazy.

00:13:49.094 --> 00:13:52.130 align:start position:0%
It also makes me
want to call my parents

00:13:52.163 --> 00:13:55.601 size:88% position:44%
and apologize for being such
an asshole when I was a kid.

00:13:55.634 --> 00:13:57.469 align:start size:91% position:9%
- For causing them stress?
- Yeah, exactly.

00:13:57.502 --> 00:13:59.972 align:start position:0%
Like, they had enough
to deal with.

00:14:00.005 --> 00:14:04.977 align:start position:0%
I feel like I could've been
a slightly better son to them.

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:07.613 align:start size:66% position:34%
Yeah? Throughout
this entire series,

00:14:07.646 --> 00:14:11.316 align:start size:72% position:28%
we've looked at
implicit biases and AI,

00:14:11.349 --> 00:14:14.353 align:start size:69% position:31%
and disparities in
education and housing.

00:14:14.386 --> 00:14:18.290 align:start size:69% position:31%
To me, the end result
of all of those things
is this chart.

00:14:18.323 --> 00:14:20.325 align:start size:91% position:9%
It's the fact that the stress
of living in America

00:14:20.358 --> 00:14:23.495 align:start size:72% position:28%
is so, so different
depending on your race.

00:14:23.528 --> 00:14:26.999 align:start position:0%
Outside of getting this
very cryptic look at my future,

00:14:27.032 --> 00:14:31.670 align:start position:0%
is there something I can do
to not end up here when I'm 60?

00:14:31.703 --> 00:14:34.172 align:start position:0%
I think that's the tough part
about this whole episode, right?

00:14:34.205 --> 00:14:36.475 align:start size:81% position:19%
Like, how do we even start
to talk about solutions

00:14:36.508 --> 00:14:38.343 align:start size:72% position:28%
to health disparities

00:14:38.376 --> 00:14:42.481 align:start size:75% position:25%
that come from such
structural inequalities?

00:14:42.514 --> 00:14:45.350 align:start size:81% position:19%
I feel like the first step
is talking about it.

00:14:45.383 --> 00:14:47.519 size:81% position:59%
I have been in touch with
this group in Philadelphia

00:14:47.552 --> 00:14:51.657 align:start size:69% position:31%
called Black Men Heal
that pairs black men
with free therapy.

00:14:51.690 --> 00:14:53.191 align:start size:50% position:50%
So we could
go talk to them.

00:14:53.224 --> 00:14:54.526 align:start position:0%
We should definitely
go talk to them.

00:14:59.664 --> 00:15:01.466
- Hello?
- Hey.

00:15:01.499 --> 00:15:02.534
- How's it going?
- Hey, Christophe.

00:15:02.567 --> 00:15:04.002 size:84% position:42%
It's nice to meet you guys.

00:15:04.035 --> 00:15:05.203 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Nice to meet you.
- Christophe.

00:15:05.236 --> 00:15:06.571
- I'm Lee.
- Hey, Lee.

00:15:06.604 --> 00:15:08.707 align:start size:78% position:22%
- Nice to see you.
- Not too much.

00:15:08.740 --> 00:15:10.075
- Thank you for coming.
- Thanks for having us.

00:15:10.108 --> 00:15:11.343 align:start position:0% line:0%
I'm Tasnim Sulaiman.

00:15:11.376 --> 00:15:13.178 align:start position:0% line:0%
I'm the founder
of Black Men Heal,

00:15:13.211 --> 00:15:15.547
a non-profit for men of color

00:15:15.580 --> 00:15:18.350 align:start position:0%
to be able to receive
quality mental health care.

00:15:18.383 --> 00:15:21.086
I found in the research
that black men are more likely

00:15:21.119 --> 00:15:23.655 align:start size:56% position:44%
to listen
and be impacted by

00:15:23.688 --> 00:15:26.992 align:start size:72% position:28%
men within the same
age category as them
and who look like them.

00:15:27.025 --> 00:15:29.294 align:start position:0%
What feel like some of
the biggest reasons right now

00:15:29.327 --> 00:15:32.564 align:start position:0%
that people are coming in
and seeking help?

00:15:32.597 --> 00:15:36.702 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
Because of COVID, people
are quarantined by themselves.
It's a lot of job loss.

00:15:36.735 --> 00:15:39.571 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
It's a lot of grief for people
that have died from COVID,

00:15:39.604 --> 00:15:43.075
along with the racial uprisings
around George Floyd's death.

00:15:43.108 --> 00:15:45.577 align:start size:81% position:19%
So, those issues
have increased a lot more.

00:15:45.610 --> 00:15:47.512 align:start size:69% position:31% line:0%
Our young men of color
are at war every day.

00:15:47.545 --> 00:15:51.083 line:0%
With this year, that war
has been put on the front page

00:15:51.116 --> 00:15:52.584 line:0%
over and over again,

00:15:52.617 --> 00:15:54.720 align:start size:72% position:28%
so they're experiencing
heightened anxiety.

00:15:54.753 --> 00:15:57.255 line:0%
So many guys were applying,

00:15:57.288 --> 00:16:00.158 line:0%
so we had to think
of something virtually to do.

00:16:00.191 --> 00:16:03.061
So we started hosting
a segment based on

00:16:03.094 --> 00:16:04.663
the different things
that were going on
in our country.

00:16:04.696 --> 00:16:07.332
You know,
police brutality, grief.

00:16:07.365 --> 00:16:09.134
Even for me, I--

00:16:11.269 --> 00:16:12.604 align:start size:47% position:53%
Taj:
It's good, bro.

00:16:15.373 --> 00:16:17.676
Like, I lost
a dear friend on Friday.

00:16:17.709 --> 00:16:20.379
And that Sunday,
we talked about the pain

00:16:20.412 --> 00:16:22.681
of losing somebody
and what it means,

00:16:22.714 --> 00:16:26.118
and the fulfillment
and the value and the respect

00:16:26.151 --> 00:16:29.521
that I got was other people
in the room was having
the same thoughts

00:16:29.554 --> 00:16:33.692 align:start size:59% position:41%
or the same pain,
and they found out
they weren't alone.

00:16:33.725 --> 00:16:36.762
You know, and I was talking
to Taj before this,

00:16:36.795 --> 00:16:40.132
like, do men really know
how to cry?

00:16:40.165 --> 00:16:43.135 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Mm-hmm.
- Because we've been told
  so many different times

00:16:43.168 --> 00:16:45.370 align:start position:0%
that, "Yo, just suck it up.
Don't show no tears."

00:16:45.403 --> 00:16:49.608
For me, there's a sense
of a release.

00:16:49.641 --> 00:16:53.645 align:start size:69% position:31%
How are you all going
about arming these men

00:16:53.678 --> 00:16:56.314
with the language to articulate
what they're going through

00:16:56.347 --> 00:16:58.483
in a world
that takes advantage of you,

00:16:58.516 --> 00:17:02.120 align:start size:88% position:13%
especially when
you're a black male and you
present that vulnerability?

00:17:02.153 --> 00:17:05.657 align:start size:66% position:34%
One man being willing
to step into a space
of vulnerability,

00:17:05.690 --> 00:17:08.727 align:start size:81% position:19%
it's like putting
your hand out and inviting
another man to come in.

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:11.630 align:start size:66% position:34%
I think vulnerability
is like the new sexy.

00:17:11.663 --> 00:17:14.366 align:start size:66% position:34%
Like, I think that's
a very powerful bond.

00:17:14.399 --> 00:17:16.068 align:start size:81% position:19%
Thank you, Taz, for saying
vulnerability is sexy.

00:17:16.101 --> 00:17:19.171
- Yes. It's the new sexy.
- I feel good about it.

00:17:19.204 --> 00:17:21.206 align:start size:91% position:9%
- ( laughter )
- I'm bringing sexy back.

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:26.244
But, um, that transparency,
it is such a tough thing,

00:17:26.277 --> 00:17:29.748 align:start size:81% position:19%
you know, growing up
having to put on a certain
facade, a certain image.

00:17:29.781 --> 00:17:31.516 align:start size:53% position:47%
And I think about
Black Men Heal,

00:17:31.549 --> 00:17:34.820
even the pairing up
with a therapist of color,

00:17:34.853 --> 00:17:37.556
somebody that you can
actually identify with--

00:17:37.589 --> 00:17:40.525
'cause if you go see somebody,
let's say, that's not black,

00:17:40.558 --> 00:17:44.362
I know for me,
I'm already stereotyping
what you think of me,

00:17:44.395 --> 00:17:46.431
so there is a fear right there.

00:17:46.464 --> 00:17:48.834
And to be able to see
a therapist of color

00:17:48.867 --> 00:17:50.836
gives you the freedom
to open up.

00:17:50.869 --> 00:17:52.838
I actually have
a question for you.

00:17:52.871 --> 00:17:55.507 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Oh, yeah?
- Have you ever taken
  into consideration

00:17:55.540 --> 00:17:57.709
race of the therapist
and been like,

00:17:57.742 --> 00:18:00.378 align:start size:81% position:19%
"I don't feel
comfortable talking to you
because you're not--"

00:18:00.411 --> 00:18:02.581 align:start position:0%
No, I mean, I think
that's something

00:18:02.614 --> 00:18:05.150 align:start position:0%
that I have the privilege
of not thinking about
a lot of the time.

00:18:05.183 --> 00:18:08.353 size:81% position:41%
When I do go to the doctor
or other health services,

00:18:08.386 --> 00:18:11.223
those are not crossing my mind.

00:18:11.256 --> 00:18:14.726
I know a lot of times
we talk about the systematic
racism and, you know,

00:18:14.759 --> 00:18:17.596
the black community is like,
"Why don't you understand?"

00:18:17.629 --> 00:18:21.867
What we have to realize,
there's a reason why somebody
might not understand,

00:18:21.900 --> 00:18:24.569
'cause they don't see
through that same lens.

00:18:24.602 --> 00:18:26.738 size:78% position:39%
You mentioned that there
are people as young as 18

00:18:26.771 --> 00:18:29.841 align:start position:0%
and as old as 85
coming to Black Men Heal.

00:18:29.874 --> 00:18:33.211 align:start position:0%
When you compare people
from generations so far apart,

00:18:33.244 --> 00:18:34.880 align:start position:0%
what similarities
and differences do you see

00:18:34.913 --> 00:18:37.749 align:start position:0%
in the things that
they're struggling with?

00:18:37.782 --> 00:18:39.784 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Tons of similarities.
- More similarities than--

00:18:39.817 --> 00:18:42.487
Taj: Yeah, and that's the hope
and the inspiration right there,

00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:45.257
because you may hear
an older gentleman talk about

00:18:45.290 --> 00:18:47.692
a scenario or a situation
that he's gone through,

00:18:47.725 --> 00:18:49.594
but you're in it right now.

00:18:49.627 --> 00:18:51.429 align:start size:81% position:19%
And so that lets you know,

00:18:51.462 --> 00:18:53.798
"Hey, if he got through it,
I'ma get through it as well."

00:18:53.831 --> 00:18:56.434
Black Men Heal saved my life,

00:18:56.467 --> 00:18:58.870 align:start size:53% position:47%
and it saved me
from my own self.

00:18:58.903 --> 00:19:01.806
It's the tools that I've learned
through Black Men Heal

00:19:01.839 --> 00:19:05.244 align:start size:69% position:31%
that kept me stable,
that kept me grounded.

00:19:07.478 --> 00:19:09.281
When I first started
reporting this story,

00:19:09.314 --> 00:19:13.285
I felt shocked to see
that interpersonal racism

00:19:13.318 --> 00:19:15.620
<i> and the consequences of being
 on the receiving end of it</i>

00:19:15.653 --> 00:19:18.223
<i> could be measured
 in this way,</i>

00:19:18.256 --> 00:19:21.259
<i> and I felt curious
 to understand it better.</i>

00:19:21.292 --> 00:19:22.427
But there's one thing
that Dr. Williams told me

00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:23.762
that I can't stop
thinking about.

00:19:23.795 --> 00:19:25.630 align:start size:69% position:31%
Williams: I think
for many white persons

00:19:25.663 --> 00:19:27.599 align:start size:66% position:34%
in the United States,
it is a surprise.

00:19:27.632 --> 00:19:29.968
But for many persons of color,

00:19:30.001 --> 00:19:32.304
it's not a surprise.

00:19:32.337 --> 00:19:35.941
But for some,
it is a powerful affirmation

00:19:35.974 --> 00:19:41.479
that the experiences
we have had, um, are real,

00:19:41.512 --> 00:19:44.449
and they actually
can be studied.

00:19:44.482 --> 00:19:47.953
Whether you feel surprised
or validated by this research

00:19:47.986 --> 00:19:50.889
is going to depend
on what you've experienced
in your own lifetime.

00:19:50.922 --> 00:19:54.326 size:84% position:58%
I can quote, like, verbatim

00:19:54.359 --> 00:19:58.830 align:start size:69% position:31%
racist incidents
that took place about,
like, 10 years ago.

00:19:58.863 --> 00:20:01.833 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so hearing that there's
research around this

00:20:01.866 --> 00:20:05.403 align:start size:69% position:31%
just makes me feel--
I feel relieved.

00:20:05.436 --> 00:20:06.705 align:start size:78% position:22%
Because it's like, great,
we're talking about this.

00:20:06.738 --> 00:20:08.907 size:88% position:56%
These numbers are out there.

00:20:08.940 --> 00:20:11.910
Christophe:
<i> Racism created
 the racial health gap,</i>

00:20:11.943 --> 00:20:14.479
<i> but anti-racism
 can help close it.</i>

00:20:14.512 --> 00:20:17.916
<i> That work, the work that falls
 on each one of us every day,</i>

00:20:17.949 --> 00:20:19.985
<i> is a matter
 of public health.</i>

00:20:20.018 --> 00:20:22.954
We tend to put ourselves
in the shoes of someone

00:20:22.987 --> 00:20:26.691
who belongs to the same
racial group that we're in.

00:20:26.724 --> 00:20:28.727
<i> One of the challenges we face</i>

00:20:28.760 --> 00:20:33.365
<i>is how do we tell the stories
of disadvantaged populations--</i>

00:20:33.398 --> 00:20:34.699
<i> the discrimination,
 the stress,</i>

00:20:34.732 --> 00:20:36.668
<i> the lower income,
 and poverty.</i>

00:20:36.701 --> 00:20:38.603
<i> They're living
 in poor neighborhoods.</i>

00:20:38.636 --> 00:20:41.606
<i> How can we tell those stories
 in ways that connect</i>

00:20:41.639 --> 00:20:44.276
<i> with the average American</i>

00:20:44.309 --> 00:20:46.978
<i> so that they feel empathy
 and then motivated</i>

00:20:47.011 --> 00:20:51.917
<i>to support policy interventions
 to address it?</i>

00:20:53.951 --> 00:20:56.755 align:start position:0% line:0%
Oh, no, we messed it up.
Oh, no!

00:20:56.788 --> 00:20:58.023 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
- ( laughs )
- Carrie, did that hit you?

00:20:58.056 --> 00:20:59.524 line:0%
- Carrie: No.
- Thank God.

00:20:59.557 --> 00:21:03.028 align:start position:0% line:0%
So, if you're Hispanic,
you can--

00:21:03.061 --> 00:21:05.397 align:start size:75% position:25% line:0%
Why is that one smoking?

00:21:05.430 --> 00:21:07.565 align:start position:0% line:0%
Oh! Where am I?

00:21:07.598 --> 00:21:08.934 align:start position:0% line:0%
Okay, well,
this is embarrassing.

